#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055579} :activity/id #uuid "4dd99c93-ce32-53a9-b191-e8bddb7f8f7f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048778 :topic/id #uuid "bdf6a63d-1183-5d54-9223-faab1de59819" :topic/math-academy-id 60} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056005 :tutorial/content "A **compound statement** is a sentence that consists of two or more statements separated by logical connectors, such as the word \"and\" or the word \"or.\"\n\nFor example, the following sentence is a compound statement:\n\n$P: 2$ is an integer **and** $2$ is positive\n\nThe first statement is \"$2$ is an integer,\" and the second is \"$2$ is positive.\" These two statements are separated by the logical connector \"and.\"\n\nThe following sentence is also a compound statement:\n\n$7$ is odd **or** $7$ is even\n\nIn this case, the first statement is \"$7$ is odd,\" and the second statement is \"$7$ is even.\" These two statements are separated by the logical connector \"or.\"\n\nWe can express the logical connectives \"and\" and \"or\" using symbols:\n\n- The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land$\". Notice that it resembles \"A\" for \"And.\"\n- The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nLet's translate the following sentence into symbolic form:\n\n$2$ is an integer **and** $2$ is positive\n\nTo start, we introduce the letters $A$ and $B$ to denote each individual statement:\n\n- $A:$ $2$ is an integer\n- $B:$ $2$ is positive\n\nThen, our sentence becomes\n\n$$\\underbrace{2 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is an integer} }_A \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{2 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is positive} }_B,$$\n\nthat is, $A \\land B.$\n\nFinally, note the following terminology:\n\n- The statement \"$P$ and $Q$\" is called the **conjunction** of the statements $P$ and $Q.$\n- The statement \"$P$ or $Q$\" is called the **disjunction** of the statements $P$ and $Q.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4570766f-a355-5a80-9756-cd94d76fed9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7962 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a197e18-2f93-5927-89e7-71b02eeb66ff" :step/math-academy-id 153494 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054641 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054365 :question/id #uuid "a8c9f90f-3c97-5a01-99b5-9a9f8b3eef66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12677"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93bc0caa-2352-5d02-9ccf-7267dff4be2c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049002}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389768}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Compound Statements in Symbolic Form"} :step/id #uuid "9a7b96e3-5988-5977-a20a-8a97b636978b" :step/math-academy-id 153495 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054642 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054366 :question/id #uuid "e5a60113-14a0-5468-93b1-7f0eaa6e9a90" :question/math-academy-id "e-12678"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2ea816-3a62-5167-9f08-17ebcf97c22e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389848}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Nested Compound Statements in Symbolic Form"} :step/id #uuid "c089550c-a72d-5a29-a351-4052cea239c4" :step/math-academy-id 153498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056029 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"and\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\land B$ is true if $A$ is true **and** $B$ is true. Otherwise, it is false.*\n\nLet's determine the truth values of the following compound statement:\n\n$2$ is positive **and** $2$ is even.\n\nWriting this in symbols, we have the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nor, simply\n\n$$A\\land B.$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A∧B≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIn this context, the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\"\n\nLet's now consider a similar compound statement but with the $2$ replaced with a $3$ in the second statement.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$A\\land C$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $C$ is false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A∧C≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e16d77df-210e-5934-90ce-44b096916261" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8142 :tutorial/title "Truth Values of Statements Containing the \"And\" Connective"} :step/id #uuid "237b49c1-2062-5a12-862e-cc6120dfe275" :step/math-academy-id 153501 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056072 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"or\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. Otherwise, it is false.*\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, consider the following statement: $$\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}$$ Since $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true: $$\\begin{aligned}A∨B≡\\text{true}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}$$\n- Next, consider the following statement: $$\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}$$ Since $A$ is true, this compound statement is true: $$\\begin{aligned}A∨C≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}$$\n- Now, consider the following: $$\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}$$ Since $B$ is true, this compound statement is true: $$\\begin{aligned}D∨B≡\\text{false}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}$$\n- Finally, consider the following: $$\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}$$ Since $D$ and $C$ are both false, this compound statement is false: $$\\begin{aligned}D∨C≡\\text{false}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b0d24cd-f605-59b2-9f16-f053e9b74e8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9044 :tutorial/title "Truth Values of Statements Containing the \"Or\" Connective"} :step/id #uuid "1023b546-9b0c-52d3-9454-3a5adbb094ec" :step/math-academy-id 153502 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054643 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054368 :question/id #uuid "6497cb54-2453-5dd7-8b97-09e06280d774" :question/math-academy-id "e-12693"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ab93ca-16ca-5e57-835f-036b59f25d63" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389734}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Values of Compound Statements"} :step/id #uuid "96ad2c2e-7a64-57d5-9f83-02022e8509c3" :step/math-academy-id 153503 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054389 :question/id #uuid "9209c4e5-30e0-5933-9c41-32750b5301ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-12895"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c15e521-f848-5d9c-ba06-00550277b8cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389825}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Values of Complex Statements"} :step/id #uuid "3cd4f3c8-8abb-53a5-a3a4-5df352b82883" :step/math-academy-id 153506}] :activity/title "The \"And\" and \"Or\" Connectives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054914}]}
